EXT. GASLIT ALLEY - LONDON - NIGHT (1881)

Fog swallows the lamps. Wet cobbles. Somewhere a dog howls.

TOBY ASHWORTH, 12, a lamplighter's boy, balances on a ladder against a post, poking his long pole at a stubborn flame. MRS. FINNEGAN, 60s, a flower seller with a basket, hurries past, head down. Neither sees the third figure until it steps out of the fog: CONSTABLE QUILL, 30s, in a caped coat, whistle between his teeth.

QUILL
You there. Boy.

Toby fumbles. The pole slips. The lamp blazes and the three of them are suddenly lit, a tableau. Mrs. Finnegan stops. Quill stops. Toby holds the ladder with both hands.

QUILL (CONT'D)
Have you seen a woman in a green shawl?

TOBY
(too quickly)
No, sir.

Mrs. Finnegan's basket tips. Violets spill. She and Toby both lunge for them, and Quill grabs her arm. The ladder tilts. Toby clings to the post and kicks free. The constable's whistle falls from his teeth. A green shawl peeks out from under the flowers.

MRS. FINNEGAN
(a whisper)
Run.

Toby drops from the ladder and bolts. Mrs. Finnegan hurls her basket at the constable. Violets fly. Quill stumbles back. All three vanish into the fog in three directions.
